			<!-- ==================================================== states
			     Every async surface has to render these three, and a system
			     that does not own them lets every consumer invent them —
			     usually in colour, or as an unguarded spinner. -->
			<section id="states" class="cm-section cm-section--pad">
				<SectionHead title="states">
What a surface shows before it has data, and after it fails. Status is carried by a glyph and a word, never by hue: this system has no chroma to carry it, and greyscale print still reads correctly.
				</SectionHead>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--flush">empty</h3>
				<div class="cm-state">
					<span class="cm-state__mark" aria-hidden="true">Ø</span>
					<p class="cm-state__title">Nothing here yet</p>
					<p class="cm-state__body">
						An empty surface still needs a shape. A blank div reads as a broken
						page; this one says the same thing on purpose.
					</p>
					<div class="cm-state__actions">
						<a class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm" href="#states">create the first one</a>
					</div>
				</div>

								<!-- Progress is carried by weight and inversion, the two cues
				     the rest of the system already speaks: done keeps a strong
				     border, current fills. The connector is a 1px rule that
				     disappears when the row stacks. Clicking a step rewrites
				     all three states at once, so "done" and "current" can
				     never disagree. -->
				<h3 class="cm-kicker">steps</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub">
					A wizard's progress line. State is legible in greyscale and
					to a screen reader, because <code>aria-current="step"</code>
					moves with the class instead of being set once and left to rot.
				</p>
				<ol class="cm-stepper" data-cm-stepper>
					<li><button type="button" class="cm-step is-done"><span class="cm-step__marker" aria-hidden="true">✓</span><span class="cm-step__label">plan</span></button></li>
					<li><button type="button" class="cm-step is-current" aria-current="step"><span class="cm-step__marker" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span class="cm-step__label">build</span></button></li>
					<li><button type="button" class="cm-step"><span class="cm-step__marker" aria-hidden="true">3</span><span class="cm-step__label">verify</span></button></li>
					<li><button type="button" class="cm-step"><span class="cm-step__marker" aria-hidden="true">4</span><span class="cm-step__label">ship</span></button></li>
				</ol>

<h3 class="cm-kicker">loading</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub">
					Two different jobs. A <strong>skeleton</strong> is for a surface whose
					shape you already know; a <strong>spinner</strong> is for an action in
					flight. Both stop under <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>.
				</p>
				<div class="cm-skeleton" aria-hidden="true" style="max-width:22rem">
					<div class="cm-skeleton__block"></div>
					<div class="cm-skeleton__line"></div>
					<div class="cm-skeleton__line cm-skeleton__line--w60"></div>
					<div class="cm-skeleton__line cm-skeleton__line--w40"></div>
				</div>
				<p class="cm-spinner-row" role="status" style="margin-top:var(--space-4)">
					<span class="cm-spinner" aria-hidden="true"></span>
					working…
				</p>

				<!-- the other face: a GLYPH that is already the right shape. A ring
				     is for a surface with nothing to show yet; a refresh arrow inside
				     its own button should keep being an arrow and just turn. Same
				     keyframes, so the two cannot drift out of step side by side. -->
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
					<strong>cm-glyph-spin</strong> turns a glyph that is already the right
					shape - a refresh arrow inside its own button - where a
					<strong>spinner</strong> would draw a second ring beside the label.
				</p>
				<p style="margin-top:var(--space-2)">
					<button type="button" class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm">
						<svg class="cm-glyph-spin" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24"
							fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true">
							<path d="M21 12a9 9 0 1 1-3-6.7" />
							<polyline points="21 3 21 9 15 9" />
						</svg>
						refreshing…
					</button>
				</p>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker">alerts</h3>
				<div style="display:grid;gap:var(--space-3);max-width:34rem">
					<div class="cm-alert cm-alert--ok" role="status">
						<span class="cm-alert__mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
						<div class="cm-alert__body">
							<p class="cm-alert__title">saved</p>
							<p class="cm-alert__text">The runtime is bundled and the toggle persists under this site's own key.</p>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-alert cm-alert--warn" role="status">
						<span class="cm-alert__mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
						<div class="cm-alert__body">
							<p class="cm-alert__title">vendored copy is stale</p>
							<p class="cm-alert__text">A consumer still holds an old copy of the layers. Run the sync check before trusting its CSS.</p>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-alert cm-alert--err" role="alert">
						<span class="cm-alert__mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
						<div class="cm-alert__body">
							<p class="cm-alert__title">build failed</p>
							<p class="cm-alert__text">Two of eleven spacing bars measured the wrong length. A specimen that lies is worse than no specimen.</p>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-alert cm-alert--info" role="status">
						<span class="cm-alert__mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
						<div class="cm-alert__body">
							<p class="cm-alert__title">3 hosts re-synced</p>
							<p class="cm-alert__text">dev-blog, links and nodeterm carry the new runtime. info is the quiet one: the same grey as a warning, a different mark.</p>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-alert cm-alert--loading" role="status">
						<span class="cm-alert__mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
						<div class="cm-alert__body">
							<p class="cm-alert__title">syncing the fleet</p>
							<p class="cm-alert__text">loading is the only mark that MOVES: a spinning ↻ under the same reduced-motion block as the spinner.</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker">table</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub">
					Data, not prose. The scroll wrapper is part of the component: a wide table
					that overflows the page is the most common way a mono layout breaks on a
					phone, so the component refuses to let it.
				</p>
				<div class="cm-scroll-fade-x cm-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="token cost by layer">
					<table class="cm-table">
						<thead>
							<tr><th>layer</th><th>file</th><th class="num">lines</th><th class="num">touches</th></tr>
