<section id="code" class="cm-section cm-section--pad">
				<SectionHead title="code">
A <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> is an element default, so it is on-brand before you add a class. <code>.cm-codebar</code> is the wrapper that gives it a label row and a home for a copy button, and <code>.cm-copy</code> is the button. Press one: the label changes, and the state class is the only feedback &mdash; this system has no chroma, so the change has to be legible in greyscale.
				</SectionHead>

				<CodeBlock
					id="demo-install"
					lang="bash"
					code={`curl -fsSL https://example.com/install.sh | sh`}
				/>

				<CodeBlock
					id="demo-config"
					lang="typescript"
					code={`export const site = {
  name: 'oem',
  theme: 'dark',
} satisfies SiteConfig;`}
				/>

				<!-- The guard. Shown because the wiring is the whole point:
				     a consumer that owns its own <head> must inline this
				     file, and the ?raw import is the only shape that keeps
				     it ahead of the stylesheets. -->
				<CodeBlock
					id="demo-guard"
					lang="astro"
					code={`---
// <head> runs before the runtime bundle exists, so it cannot ask the
// runtime for its keys. Inline the shipped file instead of hand-rolling
// one, or a theme saved under a legacy key is missed and every returning
// light-theme visitor gets a black flash.
import themeGuard from '../js/cli-mono-theme-guard.js?raw';
---
<head>
  <!-- before <meta charset>, before every stylesheet -->
  <script is:inline set:html={themeGuard} />
</head>`}
				/>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker">states</h3>
