																							<td>$1.20</td>
																							<td>31 t/s, first token 380ms</td>
																						</tr>
																						<tr>
																							<td>qwen3.8-27b</td>
																							<td>local</td>
																							<td>local</td>
																							<td>18 t/s with thinking disabled</td>
																						</tr>
																					</tbody>
																				</table>
																			</div>
																		</section>
																	</section>
			<!-- ==================================================== layout
			     The two page shapes that are not a single column: a lede
			     under the head, and a main block with a metadata aside.
			     Both were hand-rolled per consumer with a raw `ch` in
			     each file, so a change to reading measure never reached
			     any of them. The specimens below are drawn FROM the
			     tokens they document. -->
			<section id="layout" class="cm-section cm-section--pad">
				<SectionHead title="layout">
<code>.cm-lede</code> is the opening sentence under a page head, and <code>.cm-split</code> puts a metadata column beside a content block. The measure comes from <code>--measure</code>, not a number typed next to it, so changing the reading width is a token change. The split stacks below 700px on purpose: two columns of text on a phone is two unreadable columns.
				</SectionHead>

									<!-- A carousel is a scrollport: swipe first, buttons second,
				     and both drive the same scroll position, so there is no
				     transform to desync from what the finger actually did.
				     The page marks are the position readout; each one is a
				     full --tap target with an 8px square painted inside it. -->
				<h3 class="cm-kicker">resizable panels</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub">
					The boundary is <code>--cm-resize</code>, a percentage, so the split
					survives a reflow instead of snapping back to pixels the window no
					longer has. The handle is the seam itself: a hairline pair 9px wide
					(a 1px seam cannot be grabbed), dragged with pointer capture and
					nudged by 16px steps with the arrow keys. Below 640px the group
					stacks and the same handle drags vertically.
				</p>
				<div class="cm-resize" data-cm-resize style="--cm-resize: 58%;">
					<section class="cm-resize__panel">
						<p class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--flush">index</p>
						<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin:0">
							A narrow column of metadata. It takes the percentage; the
							sibling takes whatever is left, which is why neither panel
							declares a width of its own.
						</p>
					</section>
					<div class="cm-resize__handle" role="separator" tabindex="0"
						aria-orientation="vertical" aria-valuenow="58"
						aria-valuemin="20" aria-valuemax="80" aria-label="resize panels">
					</div>
					<section class="cm-resize__panel">
						<p class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--flush">content</p>
						<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin:0">
							Drag the seam, or focus it and press an arrow key: the step
							is 16px in either direction and the range is the same
							aria-valuemin/valuemax the separator reports.
						</p>
					</section>
				</div>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker">carousel</h3>
				<div class="cm-carousel" data-cm-carousel aria-label="oem/ui surfaces">
					<div class="cm-carousel__track" tabindex="0">
						<div class="cm-carousel__slide">
							<p class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--flush">01 / tokens</p>
							<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin:0">Radius, rhythm, ink and line are tokens. A component never names a number it could take from one.</p>
						</div>
						<div class="cm-carousel__slide">
							<p class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--flush">02 / states</p>
							<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin:0">Empty, loading and error are designed surfaces, not afterthoughts that show a spinner.</p>
						</div>
						<div class="cm-carousel__slide">
							<p class="cm-kicker cm-kicker--flush">03 / layers</p>
							<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin:0">Stacking is a named scale. One line retunes what sits above what, instead of hunting eleven literals.</p>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-carousel__bar">
						<button type="button" class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm" data-cm-carousel-prev>prev</button>
						<div class="cm-carousel__pages" aria-label="slide">
							<button type="button" class="cm-carousel__page" aria-label="slide 1" aria-current="true"></button>
							<button type="button" class="cm-carousel__page" aria-label="slide 2"></button>
							<button type="button" class="cm-carousel__page" aria-label="slide 3"></button>
						</div>
						<button type="button" class="cm-btn cm-btn--sm" data-cm-carousel-next>next</button>
					</div>
				</div>

<!-- `.cm-shell--rail` centres a PROSE measure in the space
					     beside the rail, which is right for a content page. An
					     application shell wants the whole column instead: with
					     the prose measure the page slid right as the window
					     widened (measured x=336 at 1280px, x=416 at 1440px),
					     because `margin-inline: auto` re-centres the capped
					     child in whatever room is left. `.cm-shell--rail--fill`
					     releases the cap AND the fixed `width: var(--maxw)`, so
					     the column is pinned to the rail's padding edge and
					     uses all the rest. Rendered nested here because the
					     showcase's own shell is the prose variant. -->
					<div class="cm-shell cm-shell--rail cm-shell--rail--fill"
						 style="border:1px solid var(--line);margin-bottom:var(--space-5)">
						<div style="padding:var(--space-4)">
							<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin:0">
								An application column. It fills the space beside the rail
								instead of being centred in it, so the left edge stays
								put at every window width.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>

				<div class="cm-spec" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-6)">
					<div class="cm-spec__row">
						<span class="cm-spec__label"><code>--measure</code></span>
						<span class="cm-spec__sample"><span class="cm-lede" style="margin:0">The widest a reading column gets, in characters.</span></span>
						<span class="cm-spec__val"><code>68ch</code></span>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-spec__row">
						<span class="cm-spec__label"><code>--measure-narrow</code></span>
						<span class="cm-spec__sample"><span class="cm-lede" style="margin:0;max-width:var(--measure-narrow)">A compact block, not re-wrapped.</span></span>
						<span class="cm-spec__val"><code>34ch</code></span>
					</div>
					<!-- The inline row's title column. It exists so a list
					     reads as a table: every excerpt starts at the same
					     offset instead of wherever the previous title
					     happened to end. Below 845px, and in the
					     1000-1055px rail band, the excerpt is handed off
					     rather than squeezed to a sliver — so this
					     specimen carries a live readout of whether the
					     column is currently in play, which is the whole
					     question a reader of this token should ask. -->
					<div class="cm-spec__row" data-spec="measure-title">
						<span class="cm-spec__label"><code>--measure-title</code></span>
						<span class="cm-spec__sample">
							<span class="cm-lede" style="margin:0;max-width:var(--measure-title)">The title column of a list row.</span>
						</span>
						<span class="cm-spec__val"><code>42ch</code></span>
					</div>
				</div>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker">lede</h3>
				<p class="cm-lede">
					Read this before the rest. It is wider and dimmer than the
					body text, so the eye takes the sentence in once and then
					moves down to the content instead of reading the page twice.
				</p>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker">key-value, wrapped pairs</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-4)">
					HTML lets you group <code>dt</code>/<code>dd</code> inside a
					<code>div</code> when a row needs a control of its own - a link
					around the whole row, a toggle, a popover trigger. The
					wrapper is a real element, and a real element inside a
					<code>display:grid</code> parent becomes a grid
					<em>item</em>, which is how a two-column list silently turns
					into a grid of stacked blocks. The library takes the
					wrapper out of the layout with <code>display:contents</code>.
					Both lists below are the same markup; the second one only
					adds the wrapper. <strong>Check them at a desktop width</strong>
					- on a phone both stack to one column and look identical, so
					a narrow-viewport pass cannot see this at all.
				</p>
				<div class="cm-split">
					<div>
						<h4 class="cm-kicker">direct</h4>
						<dl class="cm-kv">
							<dt>role</dt><dd>engineer</dd>
							<dt>since</dt><dd>2014</dd>
							<dt>based</dt><dd>remote</dd>
						</dl>
					</div>
					<div>
						<h4 class="cm-kicker">wrapped</h4>
						<dl class="cm-kv">
							<div><dt>role</dt><dd>engineer</dd></div>
							<div><dt>since</dt><dd>2014</dd></div>
							<div><dt>based</dt><dd>remote</dd></div>
						</dl>
					</div>
				</div>
					<div>
						<h4 class="cm-kicker">flush</h4>
						<dl class="cm-kv cm-kv--flush">
							<div><dt>rows</dt><dd>4</dd></div>
							<div><dt>stack</dt><dd>v1.12</dd></div>
						</dl>
						<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin:var(--space-2) 0 0">
						<code>.cm-kv--flush</code> drops the block's own bottom margin:
						inside a dialog or a card the surrounding panel already
						provides that space, and cancelling it with an inline
						<code>style</code> is something a stylesheet cannot override
						or audit.
						</p>
					</div>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker">key-value, whole row is the link</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-4)">
					<code>.cm-kv--link</code> is the case above where the
					per-row control is an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>. Link only
					the <code>dt</code> and the target is the term - measured
					14px tall in WebKit at an iPhone viewport, a third of the
					44px floor, because <code>dt</code> is inline and
					<code>min-height</code> is ignored on an inline box. So
					the link wraps the row and owns the two columns, and the
					grid moves down one level onto it. <strong>Tap the
					gap between the term and the value</strong> and the
					row goes, not the term alone.
				</p>
				<dl class="cm-kv cm-kv--link">
					<div>
						<a href="#forms">
							<dt>role</dt>
							<dd>engineer</dd>
						</a>
					</div>
					<div>
						<a href="#forms">
							<dt>since</dt>
							<dd>2014</dd>
						</a>
					</div>
					<div>
						<a href="#forms">
							<dt>based</dt>
							<dd>remote</dd>
						</a>
					</div>
				</dl>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker">split</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-4)">
					The main block and the aside below are the same DOM at every
					width; only the column count changes. Narrow the window past
					700px and the aside drops under the content rather than
					squeezing beside it.
				</p>
				<div class="cm-split">
					<div>
						<p style="margin-top:0">
							The main column keeps the reading measure and takes the
							wider share of the two. Nothing about it changes shape
							across the breakpoint, so the paragraph you read on a
							phone is the paragraph you read on a desktop.
						</p>
						<p>
							A second paragraph, so the column is taller than a single
							block of text and the gap between the columns is doing
							visible work.
						</p>
					</div>
					<aside class="cm-split__aside">
						<dl class="cm-kv">
							<dt>role</dt><dd>engineer</dd>
							<dt>since</dt><dd>2014</dd>
							<dt>based</dt><dd>remote</dd>
						</dl>
						<a class="cm-back" href="#layout">
							<span class="cm-back__arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8592;</span>
							<span>back to layout</span>
						</a>
					</aside>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section id="surface-paint" class="cm-section cm-section--pad">
				<SectionHead title="surface">
					<code>.cm-surface</code> paints a subtree that has no <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> of its own. Every other <code>.cm-*</code> class is padding and colour <em>within</em> a surface; this one is the paint. It exists for a SCOPED adoption - one that cannot import <code>base.css</code>, because that file is bare-element rules and unlayered CSS outranks every <code>@layer</code> in a Vite build - which is what left two first-party apps hand-writing <code>style={{background:'var(--bg)', color:'var(--ink)'}}</code> at each root.
				</SectionHead>

				<!-- The specimen is an ISLAND, and deliberately so. Nested
				     inside the showcase its own <body> still paints, so a
				     class with no paint rule at all would look identical
				     here - the defect this ships a fix for is INVISIBLE on
				     a page whose body already has the colour. So the wrapper
				     carries a NEUTRAL backing that no theme in the library
				     uses, and the class under test is the only thing
				     between that backing and the reader. Remove the
				     background here and this specimen passes while
				     proving nothing; the contract test asserts it is set. -->
				<div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,17rem),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);background:#f2f0ec;padding:var(--space-4);border-radius:var(--radius);border:1px solid var(--line)">
					<div class="cm-surface" data-cm-theme="dark"
					     style="padding:var(--space-4);border-radius:var(--radius-sm);border:1px solid var(--line)">
						<p class="cm-kicker">dark island</p>
						<p style="margin:var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-4);max-width:var(--measure);font-size:var(--text-sm)">
							This block carries <code>data-cm-theme="dark"</code> and
							<code>.cm-surface</code>. Remove the class and this text
							renders as dark ink on the light neutral behind it —
							the exact black-text-on-white failure, on demand.
						</p>
						<div class="cm-btn-group">
							<button class="cm-btn cm-btn--primary" type="button">primary</button>
							<button class="cm-btn" type="button">default</button>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="cm-surface cm-surface--flat" data-cm-theme="light"
					     style="padding:var(--space-4);border-radius:var(--radius-sm);border:1px solid var(--line)">
						<p class="cm-kicker">light, flat</p>
						<p style="margin:var(--space-3) 0 0;max-width:var(--measure);font-size:var(--text-sm)">
							<code>.cm-surface--flat</code> drops the vignette for a
							consumer that already paints its own chrome, so the
							library does not composite a second treatment over it.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section id="forms" class="cm-section cm-section--pad">
				<SectionHead title="forms">
inputs are element defaults, so a bare <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> is already on-brand. <code>.cm-field</code> and friends are the layout around them.
				</SectionHead>

				<h3 class="cm-kicker">calendar</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub">
					The month is a <code>table[role=grid]</code> of buttons: every day
					takes the platform's focus and click, the arrows walk the grid,
					PageUp/PageDown change month (Shift changes year), Home/End jump
					to the ends of the week, and Escape clears. State is ARIA the
					reader hears and <code>data-cm-cal-*</code> the markup reads back -
					selection is inversion, the range is a fill, and today carries
					<code>aria-current="date"</code>. The cell is <code>--tap</code> wide
					so the square is also the touch target.
				</p>
