				<form class="cm-form" onsubmit="return false">
					<div class="cm-field-row">
						<div class="cm-field">
							<label class="cm-field__label" for="f-name">handle <span class="cm-field__req">*</span></label>
							<input id="f-name" type="text" placeholder="omiinaya" required />
							<span class="cm-field__help">lowercase, no spaces.</span>
						</div>
						<div class="cm-field">
							<label class="cm-field__label" for="f-kind">kind</label>
							<select id="f-kind" class="cm-select">
								<option>site</option>
								<option>repo</option>
								<option>service</option>
							</select>
							<span class="cm-field__help">a bare <code>select</code> is already on-brand. <code>.cm-select</code> is that same chrome as a class, which is the only form a SCOPED adoption can take - it cannot import <code>base.css</code> at all.</span>
						</div>
						<div class="cm-field">
							<label class="cm-field__label" for="f-tags">tags</label>
							<select id="f-tags" class="cm-select cm-select--multi" multiple size="4">
								<option selected>infra</option>
								<option>dns</option>
								<option>proxy</option>
								<option>storage</option>
							</select>
							<span class="cm-field__help">a list box, not a picker: it opens no popup, so <code>.cm-select--multi</code> drops the chevron the base class paints and scrolls its own overflow instead.</span>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="cm-field">
						<label class="cm-field__label" for="f-url">url <span class="cm-field__req">*</span></label>
						<input id="f-url" type="url" placeholder="https://" aria-invalid="true" aria-describedby="f-url-scheme" />
						<span id="f-url-scheme" class="cm-sr-only">include the scheme, for example https://</span>
								<span class="cm-field__error">must include the scheme.</span>
					</div>

					<div class="cm-field">
						<label class="cm-field__label" for="f-notes">notes</label>
						<textarea id="f-notes" rows="3" placeholder="what is it for?"></textarea>
					</div>

					<fieldset class="cm-field">
						<legend>visibility</legend>
						<label><input type="checkbox" checked /> listed in nav</label>
						<label><input type="checkbox" /> pinned</label>
					</fieldset>

					<fieldset class="cm-field">
						<legend>access</legend>
						<label><input type="radio" name="f-access" checked /> public</label>
						<label><input type="radio" name="f-access" /> unlisted</label>
					</fieldset>

					<!-- a control that owns an affordance. The toggle lives INSIDE
						     the field, on its right edge, so it acts on the value it reveals
						     and keeps a full tap square. It is deliberately NOT inside
						     .cm-field-row - that is the two-FIELDS grid, and a button in it
						     wraps to a second row and stops lining up with the input. -->
						<div class="cm-field">
							<label class="cm-field__label" for="f-pw">password</label>
							<div class="cm-field__control">
								<input id="f-pw" type="password" placeholder="&#8226;&#8226;&#8226;&#8226;&#8226;&#8226;&#8226;&#8226;" />
								<button type="button" class="cm-icon-btn cm-icon-btn--bare" aria-label="show password">
									<svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true">
										<path d="M1 12s4-8 11-8 11 8 11 8-4 8-11 8-11-8-11-8z" />
										<circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3" />
									</svg>
								</button>
							</div>
							<span class="cm-field__help">the toggle is <code>--tap</code> square and overlaps the field padding, so the text never runs under it.</span>
						</div>

					<!-- a slider is a value on a rail. The rail is framed for the
					     same reason the progress track is: a bare `--bg-3` rail measures
					     about 1.04:1 against its own panel. Everything else here is the
					     native input - keyboard, drag and form value all still work. -->
					<div class="cm-field">
						<label class="cm-field__label" for="f-vol">retention days <output id="f-vol-out" for="f-vol">30</output></label>
						<input id="f-vol" class="cm-slider" type="range" min="1" max="90" value="30" />
						<span class="cm-field__help"><code>input[type=range]</code> styled in place. The runtime mirrors <code>.value</code> to <code>--cm-slider</code> (the fill) and to the readout.</span>
					</div>

					<!-- one control, several parts: the addon shares a single
					     collapsed hairline with the field, so the seam reads as
					     one border rather than two boxes that happen to touch. -->
					<div class="cm-field">
						<label class="cm-field__label" for="f-host">host</label>
						<span class="cm-input-group cm-input-group--framed">
							<span class="cm-input-group__addon">https://</span>
							<input id="f-host" type="text" placeholder="ui.mrx.sh" />
						</span>
						<span class="cm-field__help"><code>.cm-input-group</code> joins an addon to a field with one collapsed border.</span>
					</div>

						<!-- Six boxes for one code. The cells are inputs, so the
						     form value and the caret are still the platform's; the
						     runtime only moves focus between them. -->
						<div class="cm-field">
							<label class="cm-field__label" for="f-otp-0">verification code</label>
							<div class="cm-otp" id="f-otp" role="group" aria-label="verification code">
								<input class="cm-otp__cell" id="f-otp-0" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="one-time-code" maxlength="1" aria-label="digit 1" />
								<input class="cm-otp__cell" inputmode="numeric" maxlength="1" aria-label="digit 2" />
								<input class="cm-otp__cell" inputmode="numeric" maxlength="1" aria-label="digit 3" />
								<input class="cm-otp__cell" inputmode="numeric" maxlength="1" aria-label="digit 4" />
								<input class="cm-otp__cell" inputmode="numeric" maxlength="1" aria-label="digit 5" />
								<input class="cm-otp__cell" inputmode="numeric" maxlength="1" aria-label="digit 6" />
							</div>
							<span class="cm-field__help"><code>.cm-otp</code> cells are plain inputs. Digits type forward, <kbd class="cm-kbd">Backspace</kbd> clears before it retreats, and pasting <code>481902</code> fills all six at once.</span>
						</div>

					<!-- A select when the list is small, a combobox when it
					     is not. Typing filters, arrows choose, Enter commits
					     and the value lands in the field. -->
					<div class="cm-field">
						<label class="cm-field__label" for="f-fw">runtime</label>
						<div class="cm-combobox" id="f-fw-wrap">
							<input class="cm-combobox__input" id="f-fw" type="text" role="combobox"
							       aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="f-fw-list"
							       aria-autocomplete="list" autocomplete="off"
							       placeholder="search runtimes…" />
							<ul class="cm-combobox__list" id="f-fw-list" role="listbox" aria-label="runtimes" hidden>
								<li class="cm-combobox__option" role="option" id="f-fw-0" data-value="node" aria-selected="false"><span>node 22</span><span class="cm-combobox__hint">lts</span></li>
								<li class="cm-combobox__option" role="option" id="f-fw-1" data-value="deno" aria-selected="false"><span>deno 2</span><span class="cm-combobox__hint">secure</span></li>
								<li class="cm-combobox__option" role="option" id="f-fw-2" data-value="bun" aria-selected="false"><span>bun 1.1</span><span class="cm-combobox__hint">fast</span></li>
								<li class="cm-combobox__option" role="option" id="f-fw-3" data-value="python" aria-selected="false"><span>python 3.12</span><span class="cm-combobox__hint">wide</span></li>
								<li class="cm-combobox__option" role="option" id="f-fw-4" data-value="rust" aria-selected="false"><span>rust 1.82</span><span class="cm-combobox__hint">steady</span></li>
								<li class="cm-combobox__option" role="option" id="f-fw-5" data-value="go" aria-selected="false"><span>go 1.23</span><span class="cm-combobox__hint">plain</span></li>
							</ul>
						</div>
						<span class="cm-field__help">typing filters the list; <code>arrow keys</code> choose and <code>enter</code> commits the value.</span>
					</div>

					<div class="cm-form__actions">
						<button type="reset" class="cm-btn">cancel</button>
						<button type="submit" class="cm-btn cm-btn--primary">save</button>
					</div>
				</form>
				<h3 class="cm-kicker">validation</h3>
				<p class="cm-head__sub">
					shadcn's forms guide is wiring, not a component - and the browser
					already ships the schema engine. <code>data-cm-validate</code> reads
					the constraints the author wrote (<code>required</code>,
					<code>type</code>, <code>pattern</code>) and renders them the way the
					guide documents: <code>aria-invalid</code> on the control,
					<code>data-invalid</code> on the wrapper, the message unhidden in
					<code>.cm-field__error</code>, focus on the first offender, a polite
					count for screen readers. Default mode is submit (their onSubmit);
					<code>data-cm-validate-mode="blur"</code> re-checks on blur (onBlur),
					<code>"input"</code> on every keystroke (onChange).
					<code>novalidate</code> silences the browser's own bubbles so this
					display is the single voice. Save proves itself with a toast; reset
					marks everything clean again.
				</p>
				<form class="cm-form" data-cm-validate data-cm-validate-toast="handle saved" novalidate>
					<div class="cm-field-row">
						<div class="cm-field">
							<label class="cm-field__label" for="v-handle">handle <span class="cm-field__req">*</span></label>
							<input id="v-handle" type="text" placeholder="omiinaya" required />
							<span class="cm-field__help">empty submit is the first thing to catch.</span>
							<span class="cm-field__error" hidden>required.</span>
						</div>
						<div class="cm-field">
							<label class="cm-field__label" for="v-mail">email <span class="cm-field__req">*</span></label>
							<input id="v-mail" type="email" placeholder="you@mrx.sh" required />
							<span class="cm-field__help">type=email carries the format rule; no email regex is re-implemented here.</span>
							<span class="cm-field__error" hidden>needs a valid address.</span>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-form__actions">
						<button type="reset" class="cm-btn">reset</button>
						<button type="submit" class="cm-btn cm-btn--primary">save</button>
					</div>
				</form>
				<form class="cm-form" data-cm-validate data-cm-validate-mode="blur" novalidate>
					<div class="cm-field">
						<label class="cm-field__label" for="v-blur">repo <span class="cm-field__req">*</span></label>
						<input id="v-blur" type="text" placeholder="owner/name" required />
						<span class="cm-field__help">mode=blur: the message appears when you LEAVE the field, not when you submit.</span>
						<span class="cm-field__error" hidden>required.</span>
					</div>
					<div class="cm-form__actions">
						<button type="reset" class="cm-btn">reset</button>
						<button type="submit" class="cm-btn cm-btn--primary">save</button>
					</div>
				</form>
			</section>

			<!-- ====================================================== search
			     Filtering a list is not editing a value, so it is not
			     .cm-inline. Ten rpm pages ship exactly this markup today
			     (SearchField.tsx), which is why the surface exists at all -
			     and why the section below is a demonstration of markup a
			     CONSUMER already emits, not one invented here.

			     Two states, because the clear button only exists once
			     there is something to clear: it is not a permanently
			     mounted control that greys out. The wide modifier is
			     demonstrated too, because rpm emits it and the library
			     never defined it until this cycle. -->
